  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single sector within a single city, which may limit generalizability to other urban land uses or geographical contexts.",
      "what": "The study evaluated 6,682 food and beverage venues in Amsterdam to determine how their digital visibility varies across Google Maps, Instagram, Facebook, and TikTok. Using computational, spatial, and qualitative methods, the authors measured spatial inequality, spatial articulation, and spatial representation across the platforms, finding surprisingly low correlations between venue visibility on each service. TikTok exhibited the highest level of visibility concentration, while platform-specific patterns aligned geographically with localized processes of gentrification and tourism.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that digital platforms do not create a uniform digital layer over cities, but instead produce distinct, non-overlapping spatial representations of the exact same urban landscape."
    }
